@rem Exercises for lesson 06_linked_lists (C++ track). Format: docs/CONTENT_FORMAT.md

@exercise cpp-list-theory-empty
@type theory
@lesson 06_linked_lists
@difficulty 1
@prompt
What does the head pointer of an empty linked list hold?
@answer
nullptr | null | NULL | a null pointer
@explain
An empty list has head == nullptr, and the last node's next pointer is nullptr, too.

@exercise cpp-list-theory-doubly
@type theory
@lesson 06_linked_lists
@difficulty 1
@prompt
A doubly linked node has two pointers besides its data. Name them.
@answer
prev and next | previous and next | prevPointer and nextPointer | prev, next | previous, next | prevPointer, nextPointer | prev and next pointer | prev and next pointers | previous and next pointer | previous and next pointers | next and prev | next and previous | next, prev | next, previous
@explain
prevPointer links to the node before, nextPointer to the node after.

@exercise cpp-list-choice-doublepointer
@type choice
@lesson 06_linked_lists
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Why does a function-based InsertAtEnd take a Node** parameter?
@choices
- Because a Node* cannot be passed to a function
* So that it can change the caller's head pointer when the list is empty
- Because Node** makes the function faster
- Because it must return two nodes
@explain
Parameters are copies. To change the caller's head pointer the function needs the address of that pointer, which is a Node**.

@exercise cpp-list-choice-index
@type choice
@lesson 06_linked_lists
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which operation is slower in a singly linked list than in an array?
@choices
- Inserting at the beginning
* Reading the element at index 500
- Deleting the first node
- Checking whether the list is empty
@explain
There is no direct access by index: you must walk from the head, node by node.

@exercise cpp-list-predict-build
@type predict
@lesson 06_linked_lists
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
};

int main() {
    Node* head = nullptr;
    for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
        Node* n = new Node{i, head};
        head = n;
    }
    for (Node* p = head; p != nullptr; p = p->next) {
        cout << p->data << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;
    while (head != nullptr) {
        Node* t = head;
        head = head->next;
        delete t;
    }
    return 0;
}
@answer
3 2 1
@explain
Every new node is put in front of the old head, so the list ends up in reverse order: 3, 2, 1.

@exercise cpp-list-fill-delete-first
@type fill
@lesson 06_linked_lists
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Complete the function that removes the first node of the list.
@code
struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
};

void deleteFirst(Node** ptrToHeadPtr) {
    Node* temp = *ptrToHeadPtr;
    *ptrToHeadPtr = temp->___;
    delete ___;
}
@answer
next
temp
@explain
Move the head to the second node first, then delete the old first node (saved in temp).

@exercise cpp-list-fill-insert-beginning
@type fill
@lesson 06_linked_lists
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Complete InsertAtBeginning.
@code
struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
};

void insertAtBeginning(Node** head, int value) {
    Node* node = new Node;
    node->data = value;
    node->next = ___;
    *head = ___;
}
@answer
*head
node
@explain
The new node points to the old first node and then becomes the new head.

@exercise cpp-list-fill-walk
@type fill
@lesson 06_linked_lists
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Complete the loop that walks to the last node.
@code
struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
};

Node* lastNode(Node* head) {
    Node* cur = head;
    while (cur->___ != nullptr) {
        cur = cur->next;
    }
    return cur;
}
@answer
next
@explain
The last node is the one whose next pointer is nullptr. (The function assumes the list is not empty.)

@exercise cpp-list-bug-delete-list
@type bug
@lesson 06_linked_lists
@difficulty 3
@prompt
This function should delete every node. Click the line that is a bug (either of the two lines involved is accepted).
@code
struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
};

void deleteList(Node* head) {
    Node* cur = head;
    while (cur != nullptr) {
        delete cur;
        cur = cur->next;
    }
}
@bugline 9 10
@explain
After delete cur the node no longer exists, so cur->next reads freed memory. Save the next pointer first, then delete the node.

@exercise cpp-list-theory-arrow
@type theory
@lesson 06_linked_lists
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which operator reads a field through a node pointer, as in  p->data  ? Write the two-character symbol.
@answer
-> | arrow | the arrow operator | arrow operator
@explain
p->data is shorthand for (*p).data: follow the pointer, then select the field.

@exercise cpp-list-choice-insert-front
@type choice
@lesson 06_linked_lists
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Why is inserting a new element at the beginning cheap in a linked list?
@choices
- Because the list keeps its elements sorted
* Because no other element has to be moved: the new node only needs its next pointer set to the old head
- Because the compiler optimises it
- Because the head pointer never changes
@explain
An array has to shift every element one place to the right. A linked list only creates one node and updates two pointers, however long the list is.

@exercise cpp-list-predict-append
@type predict
@lesson 06_linked_lists
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
};

int main() {
    int values[] = {4, 5, 6};
    Node* head = nullptr;
    Node* tail = nullptr;
    for (int v : values) {
        Node* n = new Node{v, nullptr};
        if (head == nullptr) {
            head = n;
        } else {
            tail->next = n;
        }
        tail = n;
    }
    int count = 0, sum = 0;
    for (Node* p = head; p != nullptr; p = p->next) {
        count++;
        sum += p->data;
    }
    cout << head->data << " " << tail->data << " " << count << " " << sum << endl;
    while (head != nullptr) {
        Node* t = head;
        head = head->next;
        delete t;
    }
    return 0;
}
@answer
4 6 3 15
@explain
Appending with a tail pointer keeps the order 4, 5, 6: the head is 4 and the tail is 6. The list has 3 nodes with the sum 15.

@exercise cpp-list-predict-delete-first
@type predict
@lesson 06_linked_lists
@difficulty 3
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
};

void deleteFirst(Node** head) {
    Node* temp = *head;
    *head = temp->next;
    delete temp;
}

int main() {
    Node* c = new Node{30, nullptr};
    Node* b = new Node{20, c};
    Node* a = new Node{10, b};
    Node* head = a;
    deleteFirst(&head);
    cout << head->data << " " << head->next->data << " " << (head == b) << endl;
    delete b;
    delete c;
    return 0;
}
@answer
20 30 1
@explain
deleteFirst moves the caller's head from node a (10) to node b (20) and frees a. The list is now 20, 30 and head is the same node as b.

@exercise cpp-list-bug-append-null
@type bug
@lesson 06_linked_lists
@difficulty 3
@prompt
This function appends a node to a non-empty list but crashes. Click the buggy line, then type the corrected line.
@code
struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
};

void append(Node* head, int value) {
    Node* cur = head;
    while (cur != nullptr) {
        cur = cur->next;
    }
    cur->next = new Node{value, nullptr};
}
@bugline 8
@answer
while (cur->next != nullptr) {
@explain
The loop ends only when cur is nullptr, and then cur->next is a null dereference. Stop at the last node, the one whose next is nullptr.

@exercise cpp-list-bug-insert-order
@type bug
@lesson 06_linked_lists
@difficulty 3
@prompt
After this insertAtBeginning the old list is lost and the new node points to itself. Click the line that is a bug (either of the two lines involved is accepted).
@code
struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
};

void insertAtBeginning(Node** head, int value) {
    Node* node = new Node;
    node->data = value;
    *head = node;
    node->next = *head;
}
@bugline 9 10
@explain
Once *head = node has run, *head is the new node, so node->next = *head makes the node point to itself and the old first node is lost. Set node->next first, then move the head.
